          { name: "Rural Service Reliability and Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Kentucky's agriculture, manufacturing, and small-business sectors depend on functional broadband, water systems, road maintenance, and emergency services — all of which face chronic underfunding and deterioration in regions with declining population density. The 20.9% college-attainment rate indicates a working-class constituency without the credential pathways to exit the region, meaning rural infrastructure quality directly affects their economic opportunity and quality of life. A legislative agenda that prioritizes rural infrastructure investment and broadband expansion — and that can demonstrate concrete results — speaks directly to the material constraints that shape economic outcomes in this district." },
          { name: "Unaccounted-for 5.88% Demographic Population", tag: "careful", why: "The demographic gap between reported categories and 100% represents roughly 1,600-2,000 residents whose identity, economic concerns, and civic participation are invisible in the standard data. In rural Kentucky, this gap may represent Native American communities, immigrant workers in agriculture or manufacturing, or both — constituencies whose governance needs may not be receiving constituent attention from the incumbent. A challenger who can credibly demonstrate knowledge of and engagement with this population, and can mobilize them around specific economic or civic concerns, has a pathway to organize beyond the existing Republican base in a district this closely aligned." },
          { name: "Treating a 69% Margin as a Stable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 partisan margin in a district of economically pressured homeowners and working-class families should not be interpreted as durable satisfaction with the status quo; it is instead a plurality held among voters whose material concerns may or may not be receiving adequate attention from the incumbent. Any representative who campaigns as though 69% represents a mandate rather than a conditional coalition risks losing consolidation among voters whose primary evaluation is whether government is delivering on the kitchen-table issues that matter most to them — healthcare affordability, property taxes, rural infrastructure reliability." },
          { name: "Dismissing Economic Anxiety as Parochial Local Concern", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural working-class and homeowner economic concerns — property tax burden, healthcare access, rural service viability, agricultural commodity prices — are often positioned in statewide political discourse as secondary or parochial compared to more abstract conservative principles or urban-oriented policy. In a district where 80% of voters are homeowners with direct economic stakes in these issues, messaging that frames them as peripheral rather than central to governing priorities alienates the exact constituency whose economic anxiety is driving electoral volatility. A representative who validates these concerns as legitimate and central to governing — rather than dismissing them as regionally constrained — better reflects the actual material priorities of the people who live in HD-10." }
