          "The most striking analytical gap in HD-10's data is demographic: the four reported Census categories (white, Black, Hispanic, Asian) sum to 94.12%, leaving a 5.88% unexplained gap that in rural Kentucky is almost certainly American Indian / Alaska Native population, immigrant communities in undercounted categories, or both. In a district this Republican-dominant and this homogeneous in appearance, a ~6% unaccounted-for population is not negligible — it represents roughly 1,600-2,000 residents whose civic participation, economic concerns, and voting patterns are invisible to the standard demographic frame. Whether this gap represents Native American communities with historical ties to the region, newer immigrant workers in agriculture or manufacturing, or both, the representational obligation is the same: a legislator serving this district should have specific knowledge of who these residents are, what their economic and civic concerns are, and whether the district's governance is attending to them. A 69% margin offers room for a challenger to build a viable path by consolidating opposition voters and mobilizing any constituency — including the unaccounted-for 6% — that feels neglected by the incumbent."
