          { name: "Positioning Renter Protection as 'Un-American' or 'Socialist'", tag: "avoid", why: "Abstract ideological framing that dismisses tenant protection, housing regulation, or renter advocacy as philosophical overreach fails to engage the material reality of 40% of the district's households. Renters at $54,164 income are not engaged by rhetoric about property rights in the abstract; they are engaged by whether their legislator is addressing whether they can afford housing. Ideological positioning on these issues without material policy substance will read as dismissive of the actual constituency." },
          { name: "Treating $54k Income as 'Middle Class' Without Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "Generic references to 'supporting working families' or 'strengthening the middle class' fail to address the specific cost pressures facing households at $54,164 in a high-cost state. The incumbent must demonstrate understanding of the difference between earning $54k and thriving at that income level in Kentucky's current cost environment—and must translate that understanding into specific state-level legislative proposals on healthcare, housing, childcare, or transportation rather than abstract prosperity rhetoric." }
