          { name: "Rural Employment & Economic Development in Traditional Industries", tag: "lean-into", why: "This is a working-class district with 84.4% non-college-educated adults whose economic foundation is tied to manufacturing, agriculture, energy, and small business — not knowledge economy jobs. The $58,982 median income reflects a constituency that evaluates legislative performance on whether the state is creating and protecting jobs in the sectors where they and their families actually work. Specific attention to manufacturing infrastructure, agricultural support, energy sector viability, and small business conditions is more persuasive than generic economic development messaging and directly sustains the 81% base alignment." },
          { name: "Regional Economic Isolation & Demographic Stability", tag: "careful", why: "At 92.99% white, 40.6 median age, and 19.5% renter rate, KY-HD-14 is a demographically stable, settled-in-place constituency with minimal in-migration and limited younger, college-educated population growth typical of more competitive districts. This stability is an asset for an incumbent focused on holding the seat, but it also means the district is not experiencing demographic renewal that might otherwise activate new voter constituencies or force evolution in policy positioning. The careful consideration is ensuring that the constituency does not become so isolated that it is forgotten in state legislative priorities — visibility and attention are the simplest erosion-prevention strategy." },
          { name: "Positioning Energy Policy as Environmental Extremism", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where energy policy is tied directly to family employment and household budgets, any messaging that frames energy regulation or climate policy as hostile to working-class economic interests is politically potent — but only if the incumbent is perceived as indifferent to these concerns or aligned with policies that harm them. The incumbent's protective strategy is demonstrating concrete legislative action to defend energy-sector employment and affordability, not positioning the opposition as extreme; the latter approach can inadvertently legitimize opposition messaging about the incumbent's own record." },
          { name: "Treating 81% as a Mandate for Ideological or Special-Interest Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% result is a strong mandate to serve the constituency that delivered it, not a license to pursue ideological positioning disconnected from the district's material concerns or to prioritize special interests over working-class economic security. The strategic risk is an incumbent who interprets the large margin as permission to focus on issues and coalitions outside the district's core economic interests, creating an opening for perception of abandonment. The 81% is stable as long as it is understood as a mandate to serve working-class economic security — and fragile if it is misread as a mandate for anything else." }
