          { name: "Native American Constituency & Tribal Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 96.38% of the district's population, leaving roughly 3.62% unaccounted for—a gap that in this Kentucky geography likely represents Native American residents whose civic and economic concerns require active representation. In Appalachian and southeastern Kentucky tribal territory, Native American voters have demonstrated capacity to swing competitive races when mobilized around healthcare access, economic development, and community investment priorities. Even at this percentage, authentic tribal community engagement is a governance obligation and a latent electoral asset that an incumbent cannot afford to take for granted." },
          { name: "Why Raymer Won Unopposed: Understanding the Baseline", tag: "careful", why: "Raymer's unopposed 2024 victory suggests either strong incumbent consolidation or a weak opposition recruitment environment—but neither tells the full story of HD-15's competitive baseline. In a district with only 12% college attainment, $52,440 income, and a 42.3 median age, unopposed victories can mask underlying economic anxiety or governance dissatisfaction that emerges when a credible challenger appears. Any successor or challenger must understand whether the unopposed result reflects genuine safety or simply absence of opposition, and cannot assume that demographics alone explain electoral stability in a district this economically constrained." },
          { name: "Treating Low Education Attainment as a Strategic Liability Rather Than Opportunity", tag: "avoid", why: "At 12% college attainment, KY-HD-15 is a working-class and skilled-trades constituency whose economic concerns are rooted in tangible issues—job quality, healthcare access, cost of living—not abstract policy frameworks. Any candidate who frames this district through education attainment metrics or who campaigns on raising education levels rather than on immediate economic security and jobs will misread the constituency's actual priorities. The 12% figure reflects economic structure, not intellectual capacity, and messaging that condescends to or abstracts away from concrete material concerns will alienate the voters most essential to winning here." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Homogeneity Equals Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "With 92.25% white population, 79.5% homeownership, and a median age of 42.3, the demographic profile suggests a naturally Republican constituency—but unopposed victories and demographic dominance can mask real economic grievances and governance performance concerns. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as demographically predetermined rather than as a place where material economic concerns drive voter behavior will miss the actual leverage points that determine electoral outcomes. In a district this economically constrained, voter choice is driven by whether they believe their representative is delivering on their economic security, not by inherited demographic loyalty." }
