          { name: "Education Quality and Workforce Pathways", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.3% college attainment rate is below Kentucky's state average and signals limited upward mobility for the next generation in a district where parents are evaluating whether their children have viable pathways into stable employment. Secondary education quality, vocational training access, and local job creation opportunities are the practical governance concerns for a constituency that is asking whether government is investing in their children's economic future. A representative who engages these issues with specificity—addressing both education funding and the connection to local workforce demand—better reflects what parents in KY-HD-18 actually care about." },
          { name: "Rural Infrastructure and Service Viability", tag: "careful", why: "A working-class homeowner community with modest income and a 40.7 median age depends on rural healthcare access, road maintenance, water systems, and other infrastructure that is often underfunded relative to urban areas. These are not ideological issues but practical governance concerns that affect daily household function and property value. A representative must demonstrate that rural service quality is a legislative priority, not an afterthought, and that state resources for infrastructure are being directed toward the district's needs." },
          { name: "Taking the Unopposed Result as Mandate to Govern Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 election reflects Democratic resource allocation in a deep-red state, not proof of durable electoral strength or lack of latent vulnerability. A representative who uses an uncontested race as license to govern at arm's length—assuming partisan loyalty will hold without active constituent investment—is creating opening for a future challenger who does engage. The race is not a floor; it is an opportunity to build relationships so deep that future opposition becomes implausible." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Homogeneous Despite ~4.6% Demographic Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~4.6% gap in reported demographic categories likely represents Native American or other minority residents whose constituent needs around economic mobility, service access, and community representation can become invisible when governance assumes a single narrative. In a 93.44% white district, minority constituents are easy to overlook, but active engagement—not token acknowledgment—reflects both inclusive governance and political risk management in a changing state. Neglecting this constituency creates a small but real opening for an opponent to organize around it." }
