          { name: "Treating the 65-35 Result as a Durable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65-35 result in a working-class district at $65,373 income with 40% renters and 28.1% college attainment is not a demographic lock — it is a political choice made by voters evaluating incumbent performance on economic grounds. If economic conditions tighten, if housing or healthcare costs spike, or if an opposition candidate offers credible economic messaging, the 35% opposition can expand rapidly. Any incumbent or candidate who campaigns this district as though 65-35 is a safe floor, rather than a contingent result dependent on continued working-class economic performance, will find that assumption challenged in the next competitive cycle. The strategic necessity is active consolidation of the working-class base through tangible economic policy, not quiet confidence that the partisan result is durable." }
