          { name: "Manufacturing & Blue-Collar Workforce", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 16.7% college attainment—the lowest among comparable Kentucky House districts—KY-HD-27 is dominated by workers in manufacturing, construction, logistics, and service sectors whose economic security depends on stable employment, workplace conditions, and infrastructure investment. This constituency evaluates representatives on whether they are advocating for workforce development, protecting union bargaining rights where relevant, and ensuring that state policy supports rather than undermines working-class employment. Legislative record on labor issues, apprenticeship funding, and industrial retention are the concrete signals this constituency uses to assess whether a representative is actually on their side." },
          { name: "Racial Diversity & Minority Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "With 10.47% Black, 5.62% Hispanic, and 1.43% Asian residents—totaling 17.52% minority population in a district voting 63% Republican—KY-HD-27 contains meaningful communities whose economic and civic concerns can become invisible in a landslide-margin district. These constituencies face distinct barriers in workforce access, housing discrimination, and healthcare equity that the majority white homeowner base may not prioritize. A representative who engages these communities with specific constituent attention and demonstrates legislative responsiveness to their distinct concerns can build a durable relationship that the simple partisan margin masks, and guards against future challenges where minority voter mobilization becomes a swing factor." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Permanent Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 margin in a working-class, rental-heavy district where 16.7% college attainment signals limited access to economic mobility is not a safe foundation—it is a margin contingent on the incumbent delivering materially for the constituency. Working-class voters are volatile when they believe government is not serving their economic interests, and the 37% opposition in a district this economically stressed represents a constituency that either is actively frustrated or can be mobilized to frustration if economic conditions deteriorate or the incumbent appears disconnected from their material concerns. Treating this seat as safely held rather than actively earned through constituent service is the most reliable path to losing it." },
          { name: "Framing Working-Class Concerns as Cultural Rather Than Economic", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median household income is $64,223, housing costs consume a significant share of household budgets, and 36.4% rents rather than owns, the primary constituency concern is economic survival and mobility—not abstract cultural positioning. Any candidate who campaigns this district primarily on cultural or social issues while remaining vague on economic policy, wage protection, or cost-of-living relief is misreading the constituency and creating an opening for an opponent who speaks directly to material concerns. The 63% Republican alignment reflects working-class voters' pragmatic evaluation of candidates' positions on their economic security, not ideological purity on social questions." }
