          { name: "Black Community Engagement and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 14.98% of the district, the Black community in Lexington represents roughly one in seven residents and is the second-largest demographic group after the white majority at 73.85%. The 41% Democratic share in a 59% Republican district indicates meaningful opposition concentration, and Black voter turnout and candidate preference are mathematically significant to the current margin. Black constituents in an urban district with median household income of $68,009 face specific economic mobility challenges — access to homeownership, business lending, wage equity in the skilled trades, and healthcare access — that are both genuine constituent concerns and swing factors in a district where the opposition holds 41%. Authentic engagement with Black community leadership on these material issues, combined with visible legislative attention to economic opportunity and equity, transforms this from a taken-for-granted constituency into an actively consolidated voting bloc." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Demographic Growth and Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 7.41%, the Hispanic community in HD-28 is the third-largest demographic group and significantly larger than the Asian community at 0.69%, yet often receives less political attention in urban districts where Black-white coalition dynamics dominate. Hispanic population growth in Lexington has been steady, and this constituency includes both working-class renters (consistent with the 26.1% renter rate) and small business owners with stakes in the local economy. The absence of specific Hispanic community engagement from a representative risks both failing to serve a growing constituency and missing opportunities to build a coalition that cuts across the 41% opposition. This is not a zero-sum dynamic with Black community engagement — both are part of the same urban working-class coalition — but it requires deliberate attention rather than assumption that Hispanic voters are already consolidated." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Durable Majority in an Urban District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result in Lexington — Kentucky's second-largest city and a district with significant college-educated population (18.7%), urban renters (26.1%), and demographic diversity — is not a structural Republican advantage; it is a consolidated plurality that depends on active engagement with working-class economic concerns and meaningful constituent service to Black and Hispanic communities. Urban districts with these demographic profiles are inherently competitive in statewide and national cycles, and any representative who campaigns this seat as though 59% represents a stable floor rather than an actively-held coalition is vulnerable to a credible challenger who offers specific answers on housing, wages, or healthcare. The 39.7 median age signals a constituency evaluating on results and opportunity, not inherited party loyalty." },
          { name: "College-Educated Urban Progressivism as a Constituency Category", tag: "avoid", why: "While 18.7% college attainment is below the statewide average, Lexington as Kentucky's second-largest city does have a college-educated professional and academic constituency whose political preferences and material concerns differ from the working-class and skilled-trades majority. Campaigning this district as though all 59% are unified working-class conservatives risks misreading the actual coalition: the working-class economic base is consolidated, but college-educated voters in urban Lexington often have distinct priorities around education funding, environmental policy, and social issues that may not align with conservative cultural positioning. Treating educated urban professionals as a residual category to be managed rather than a genuine constituency with specific concerns is both analytically incomplete and strategically dangerous in a district where 41% opposition includes both disappointed Democrats and swing voters dissatisfied with current representation." }
