          { name: "Taking the 58% Margin as a Stable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 Republican result in a high-income, highly-educated suburban district is not a durable coalition floor — it reflects current alignment of an affluent homeowner majority that will re-evaluate if the representative is perceived as failing on education quality, fiscal responsibility, or whether diverse community members feel included in district governance. Suburban districts with similar demographics have swung 10-15 points in response to failures on education funding or perceptions of exclusionary governance. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 58% represents a safe hold rather than a margin that must be maintained through active constituent service and demonstrated competence is underestimating the volatility of affluent suburban coalitions." }
