          { name: "Treating 74% as a Permanent Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result in a district this educated and professionally engaged is not a mandate for ideological governance—it is a vote of confidence in the incumbent's competence and constituent service on specific material issues. A representative who treats the margin as permission to pursue abstract Democratic priorities rather than addressing the housing, healthcare, and urban-governance concerns of the actual constituency risks seeing that 74% erode to 65-68% in the next cycle when a credible challenger emphasizes performance on basics." },
          { name: "Assuming the 54.2% College-Educated Constituency Will Never Switch", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, higher-income professional constituencies are among the most persuadable in American politics because they evaluate on competence and results rather than cultural identity. The 26% Republican share in HD-41 demonstrates that a real minority of college-educated professionals prefer Republican governance, and in a cycle where a Democratic incumbent is seen as underperforming on housing, public safety, or economic opportunity, that 26% can grow and younger professionals can swing. This is precisely the demographic that will switch parties over functional governance failures." }
