          { name: "Cost-of-Living Economic Security for Aging, Lower-Income Homeowners", tag: "lean-into", why: "KY-HD-55 has a median age of 42, median household income of $63,101, and a homeowner-majority (75.3%) constituency whose fixed costs are rising faster than incomes. Healthcare affordability, property tax management, rural hospital viability, and retirement security are the material daily concerns for voters in this demographic profile. A legislative agenda that demonstrates concrete wins on these fronts—not abstract conservative positioning—is the primary mechanism for maintaining the 73% baseline and preventing erosion as economic pressure mounts." },
          { name: "Rural Economic Development & Workforce Pathways for Non-College-Educated Workers", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 21.6% college attainment, KY-HD-55 is a constituency where most workers will not earn a four-year degree and must build economic security through trade, technical, manufacturing, or service-sector pathways. Rural job creation, apprenticeship programs, vocational training accessibility, and support for local manufacturing or agricultural sectors are the concrete policy areas where a representative can demonstrate responsiveness to constituent economic mobility concerns. Legislative effectiveness here directly addresses why the 73% margin exists and what holds it." },
          { name: "Renter Population & Housing Cost Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "The 24.7% renter rate in a district with modest median income ($63,101) represents a constituency experiencing cost-of-living pressure distinct from homeowners. While renters are a minority of the district, they are concentrated among younger adults and lower-income households—both groups capable of organizing electoral alternatives if they conclude the incumbent is not addressing their concerns. Housing affordability and renter-focused economic policy should not be absorbed into the broader cost-of-living agenda; they require specific legislative attention." },
          { name: "Treating 73% as a Durable Mandate Rather Than a Conditional Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% result is not a mandate for inattention; it is a baseline contingent on the incumbent's active responsiveness to constituent economic security concerns. A district with modest median income, limited college attainment, and an aging population is vulnerable to economic messaging from a credible challenger who demonstrates sharper focus on cost-of-living issues. Any incumbent who consolidates the existing coalition without actively engaging opposition concerns or demonstrating legislative wins on affordability risks discovering that this margin is more fragile than comfortable partisan assumptions suggest." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 3.91% Demographic Gap or Assuming It Into the Partisan Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 96.33% of KY-HD-55's population, leaving a ~3.91% gap likely representing Native American, mixed-race, or other communities. While demographically modest, this population should not be assumed into the Republican baseline without active engagement. Direct constituent service, respectful engagement, and representation of this community's specific concerns demonstrates political maturity and prevents latent electoral vulnerability if this group perceives the incumbent as inattentive or dismissive." }
