          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40% college-educated, KY-HD-56 has one of the highest education attainment rates in Kentucky — a constituency that evaluates candidates on policy depth, rhetorical coherence, and demonstrated governance competence. College-educated voters, particularly in Republican districts, have shown increasing volatility when they perceive a candidate or party as intellectually dishonest or indifferent to evidence-based governance. The 57% margin leaves 43% opposition that likely includes college-educated voters evaluating on substance rather than party habit. A representative who can demonstrate command of complex policy issues — from tax policy to education finance to healthcare — and who engages this constituency as peers rather than as an assumed base will consolidate support more durably than one who defaults to partisan rhetoric." },
          { name: "Hispanic & Latino Community Visibility & Service", tag: "careful", why: "At 5.65% of the district, the Hispanic/Latino community is small enough to be overlooked in campaign strategy but large enough — roughly 1,400-1,600 residents depending on total population — to represent a meaningful constituency with distinct economic and social concerns. In an affluent suburban district, Hispanic residents are likely concentrated in service-sector employment (landscaping, construction, hospitality, healthcare support) with material interests in wage policy, workplace safety, and immigration enforcement balance that may differ from the Anglo homeowner majority. A representative who engages this community with specific policy attention and constituent service infrastructure demonstrates both governance integrity and competence; one who treats them as invisible or assumes their interests are absorbed into the broader community narrative risks losing credibility and mobilization potential if a competitive challenger offers specific engagement." },
          { name: "Partisan Tribalism as a Governance Framework", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a district with $84,337 income and 40% college education is not a mandate for partisan ideological governance — it is a plurality in a community where 43% actively chose an alternative and where affluent, educated voters evaluate on competence and results rather than partisan identity. Any representative who campaigns and governs as though the 57% represents a blank check for partisan orthodoxy, rather than a conditional preference for Republican governance that delivers for this specific constituency, will find that margin eroding. College-educated homeowners in high-income districts have demonstrated capacity to vote against their party when they perceive governance failure or intellectual dishonesty; treating the 57% as permission to ignore the 43% and pursue ideological priorities that don't serve this community's material interests is a strategic error." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 43% Opposition as Ideologically Alien", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 86.6% white, $84,337 median income, and 40% college-educated, the 43% Democratic opposition is not a fundamentally different constituency — it is neighbors and peers within the same affluent suburban community who have evaluated the candidates and chosen differently. This opposition likely includes college-educated professionals, homeowners, business owners, and long-term residents who share the district's basic economic interests but disagree on specific priorities, governance competence, or candidate trustworthiness. Any candidate who frames the 43% as outsiders, as ideologically unfit for this community, or as representing interests fundamentally alien to 'real' suburban voters is misdiagnosing the political landscape and will reinforce the perception that they govern only for a partisan faction rather than the entire community. The durability of a 57-43 seat depends on treating the opposition as legitimate participants in the same community conversation, not as enemies to be marginalized." }
