          { name: "College-Educated Professional Voter Engagement & Governance Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 41.1% college attainment and $99,592 median income, HD-60 has the highest concentration of college-educated, professional-class voters of any district in this group — and this constituency evaluates candidates on institutional competence, policy specificity, and demonstrated legislative effectiveness rather than partisan messaging. The 33% Democratic opposition in an affluent Republican district reflects educated voters' willingness to support Democratic candidates when they perceive superior governance competence or more credible engagement with professional-class material concerns. Any candidate who aspires to hold or expand the 67% must lead with specific, detailed legislative positions on education funding, healthcare policy, tax fairness, and infrastructure — not talking points — and must demonstrate the intellectual rigor and institutional knowledge that college-educated voters expect from someone seeking to represent them." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Costs for Affluent Aging Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 38.9 median age with 80.62% white, 17.2% renter rate, and $99,592 income, HD-60 is a community of established professional families entering the lifecycle stage where healthcare costs become a primary household concern — both for aging parents and for understanding long-term care and Medicare sustainability. The college-educated majority will evaluate state healthcare policy — Medicaid design, insurance regulation, prescription drug affordability, rural hospital viability — with the specificity of voters who understand health insurance mechanics and demographic trends. A legislative position that engages these concerns with policy depth, rather than abstract market-based positioning, resonates with a constituency that has both the income to weather healthcare cost increases and the education to understand what specific legislative choices would affect them." },
          { name: "Erosion of the 67% Margin Among College-Educated Swing Voters", tag: "careful", why: "The 33% Democratic share in a district with $99,592 income and 41.1% college attainment is elevated relative to Kentucky partisan baselines and suggests that a meaningful minority of affluent, educated professionals have concluded that Democratic governance better aligns with their material interests or institutional values. This is not an ideological rejection of conservatism but a contingent electoral choice — college-educated voters can and do switch based on candidate quality and governance competence. The 67% margin is not a stable floor; it is contingent on the incumbent or successor maintaining high credibility on institutional competence and policy substance with a constituency that has the education and resources to evaluate those claims against actual legislative records and outcomes." },
          { name: "Treating HD-60 as a Rural or Working-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-60's demographics — $99,592 income, 41.1% college, 17.2% renter rate, 80.62% white, 38.9 median age — describe an affluent, college-educated, homeowner-majority district of professional families, not a working-class or rural constituency. Any campaign communication that applies rural political messaging, assumes lower-information voters, or frames policy in terms of working-class material concerns rather than professional-class governance priorities will actively alienate the college-educated voters who constitute the district's swing margin. Candidates who cannot calibrate their communication to the actual sophistication level of their constituency will lose credibility with the educated voters whose contingent coalition support determines competitive outcomes." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 33% Opposition as Ideologically Alien Rather Than Strategically Persuadable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 33% Democratic opposition in an affluent, college-educated district represents college-educated professionals who have actively made a partisan choice — not an ideological gulf but a governance and competence assessment. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns HD-60 by consolidating the existing 67% and dismissing the opposition as fundamentally alien to the district's interests is surrendering the persuadable margin. College-educated voters respond to candidate quality, legislative accomplishment, and demonstrated competence; treating them as ideologically fixed rather than contingent on these factors is a strategic error that leaves the seat more vulnerable than its current margin should allow." }
