          "Address the college-educated professional constituency's material concerns directly: higher education affordability and state university funding, healthcare costs and Medicare sustainability for a 38.9-year-old median-age community entering peak earning years, and whether state tax policy reflects or burdens the professional workforce that drives Kentucky's economic base.",
          "Recognize that the 33% opposition is electorally significant in a wealthy, educated district — not because it represents ideological conversion, but because college-educated voters have demonstrated the capacity to switch parties when they conclude the incumbent is not delivering on competence or material governance; the 67% is not a floor but a contingent coalition requiring active stewardship.",
          "Avoid any positioning that treats HD-60 as a working-class constituency or applies rural political messaging to an affluent, professional, homeowner-majority district; voters with $99,592 income and college degrees notice and respond negatively to candidate communication that assumes lower-information voters or treats policy as secondary to partisan identity."
