          { name: "Upper-Middle-Class Stability & Quality-of-Life Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "KY-HD-64 is a $91,119 median household income district — among the highest in Kentucky — with 36.3% college attainment and a 36.9-year median age that reflects a stable, professional constituency. The 66-34 Republican margin is built on this affluent homeowner foundation's expectation of effective local governance, property value protection, and professional-class service delivery. A representative who speaks with fluency to education quality, infrastructure investment, property tax fairness, and the economic competitiveness concerns of a six-figure household base commands the credibility this constituency demands and can deepen the Republican margin further by addressing the 34% opposition as economically rational voters with addressable concerns rather than ideological opponents." },
          { name: "Education Quality & Professional Workforce Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 36.3% college-attainment rate combined with a $91,119 median income signals a district whose economic vitality depends on strong K-12 education outcomes and connection to post-secondary opportunity. This is a constituency that evaluates education policy in terms of college readiness, STEM pipeline strength, and whether local schools are positioning their children for professional advancement. School funding adequacy, teacher retention, curriculum quality, and the relationship between district secondary education and regional university/vocational pathways are not abstract policy issues — they are direct quality-of-life concerns for households whose economic security depends on sustained professional earning power. A legislator who can articulate how specific state education funding, curriculum standards, and workforce development investments strengthen this district's professional pipeline will consolidate the educated middle-class support that currently produces the 66% margin." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Potential Undercount of Minority Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 95.97% of the district population, leaving approximately a 4% gap — small but non-zero in a district where 36% have college attainment and the median income is near the state's upper quartile. This gap may represent American Indian/Alaska Native residents, immigrants, or other communities whose presence in the data is minimal but whose constituent needs around healthcare access, education quality, and economic inclusion are real. At a 66-34 margin, the Republican coalition is strong, but that 4% gap — if it represents constituents who feel overlooked or whose economic and civic concerns are not reflected in the dominant messaging — represents latent vulnerability in what appears to be a stable seat. Genuine engagement with whatever minority communities are present, rather than assumption of invisibility based on small percentages, is both a governance obligation and a risk-management investment." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as an Insurmountable Floor Rather Than an Active Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result in a district with this income and education profile is a strong margin but not a supermajority mandate, and it reflects active choice by two-thirds of voters rather than demographic inevitability. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 66% is a stable, durable base and the 34% opposition is simply 'the other side' — rather than as affluent, educated voters making rational electoral judgments about governance competence and whether their concerns are being addressed — will find that margin eroding when a credible alternative emerges. The college-educated, $91,000-income constituency is precisely the group most likely to split votes based on candidate quality and specific issue performance rather than straight-party loyalty." },
          { name: "Dismissing Upper-Middle-Class Fiscal Concerns as 'Selfish' or Secondary", tag: "avoid", why: "A $91,119 median income district with 36.3% college attainment will evaluate governance heavily on fiscal responsibility, government efficiency, and whether tax dollars are being deployed effectively. Property tax fairness, education funding adequacy, infrastructure maintenance, and the cost-effectiveness of state and local government are not narrow self-interest in this constituency — they are the practical concerns of households managing six-figure budgets where government policy directly affects disposable income and long-term financial security. Any candidate whose rhetoric frames these concerns as narrow or selfish, or who dismisses fiscal discipline as a secondary concern relative to other policy priorities, will find this constituency actively hostile. These voters view themselves as responsible stewards of their own resources and expect the same from government." }
