          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement & Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 12.79% Hispanic population in an urban, diverse, younger district represents a constituency with demonstrated persuadability in recent election cycles when presented economic alternatives or credible safety/immigration messaging. In a district where the Democratic incumbent ran unopposed, this community's specific engagement status—whether consolidated, persuadable, or mobilized for higher turnout—is unknown and therefore a critical strategic unknown. Building a durable coalition requires specific attention to Hispanic community concerns around economic mobility, immigration policy coherence, and community safety, not absorption into a generic 'younger voter' narrative." },
          { name: "Turnout & Voter Mobilization Infrastructure", tag: "careful", why: "The 28.4-year median age and 39.4% college-educated plurality create structural conditions for lower-propensity voting: younger adults, renters, and early-career professionals typically have lower turnout than older homeowners, and inherited partisan identification is weaker. Burke's unopposed status in 2024 may have suppressed voter engagement in the district; any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat must invest in systematic turnout infrastructure rather than assuming that demographic alignment with younger voters automatically translates to votes. Low turnout in this district benefits challengers because the base is less densely mobilized." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Stable Dominance", tag: "avoid", why: "Zero Republican opposition in 2024 is not a signal of unshakeable Democratic strength in HD-75—it is a signal that either the Democratic base is consolidated enough that no Republican challenger emerged, or the seat is perceived as unwinnable and no credible challenger competed. These are categorically different strategic situations. Any incumbent or candidate who interprets unopposed status as permission to neglect active constituent engagement, turnout building, or engagement with persuadable populations like the Hispanic community will find that assumption tested in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Abstract Partisan Messaging Over Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 28.4-year median age, 61.8% renters at $48,459 income, and 12.79% Hispanic population, inherited partisan loyalty is structurally weak and younger voters evaluate candidates on specific economic concerns rather than partisan identity. Any legislative message or campaign messaging that defaults to abstract conservative or liberal positioning rather than concrete solutions to housing affordability, childcare costs, wage stagnation, and community safety will fail to mobilize or persuade this constituency. The demographic composition demands economic specificity." }
