          { name: "Treating Renters as a Subordinate Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 41.4%, renters are not a minority interest group to be managed alongside a presumed homeowner-majority base—they are numerically close to a plurality and economically distinct from homeowners in their constraints and priorities. Any Democrat who frames renter advocacy as a concession to a particular interest group rather than as core constituent service will forfeit the loyalty of a constituency that has limited exit options and specific, tangible needs. Renters in this income range have constrained political choices; losing their engagement through inattention is a strategic mistake, not a necessary trade-off for homeowner support." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Diversity Precludes Coalition Competition", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's composition—71.08% white, 12.21% Black, 9.96% Latino, 2.1% Asian—contains no single demographic supermajority and no clear organizing narrative that binds these groups into an unbreakable Democratic coalition. A Republican challenger who targets the white college-educated professional base aggressively and makes credible outreach to Latino and Black communities around economic opportunity and local governance competence can generate real competition. The absence of a dominant demographic that anchors Democratic loyalty means coalition loyalty itself is the strategic variable and requires active maintenance through constituent attention and delivery." }
