          { name: "Minority Community Economic Opportunity & Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The combined 30.27% Black and 15.95% Hispanic population, plus the ~6.97% demographic gap likely including Native American residents, represents a 52-53% coalition that is the base of the 72% Democratic margin. These communities require specific legislative attention to education equity, police accountability, healthcare access, and wage-floor protections — not generic minority outreach but concrete policy delivery and community engagement that demonstrates the incumbent is accountable to these constituencies' material concerns. The incumbent's ability to sustain 72% depends on whether the Black, Hispanic, and other minority communities perceive they are being served; in a district this diverse, constituency-specific attention is not optional but foundational to coalition durability." },
          { name: "Younger Renter Turnout & Demobilization Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 35.1 median age combined with 49.6% renter concentration indicates a younger, economically mobile constituency that does not inherit partisan loyalty across generations at the same rates as older homeowner populations. In non-presidential election cycles, turnout among younger renters typically declines significantly — and in a 72-28 district, demobilization of the base is a greater risk than persuasion of Republicans. The incumbent must invest in continuous turnout infrastructure, community organizing, and direct communication with young renters in every electoral cycle, not assume that 72% is a durable coalition without active engagement; a decline in younger renter turnout from 60% to 45% across a cycle could narrow margins substantially." },
          { name: "Framing the 28% Opposition as a Persuasion Target", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 72-28 district with the demographic profile of HD-77 — working-class, renter-majority, 46% minority — the 28% Republican share reflects genuine ideological and economic difference, not soft persuadables. Republicans in this district are likely concentrated among older homeowners, business owners, and ideological conservatives who are unlikely to move to Democratic positions on housing regulation, healthcare expansion, or wage policy through persuasion campaigns. Investing limited campaign resources in persuading the 28% rather than mobilizing and consolidating the 72% base is strategically misaligned with the actual electoral math of the district." },
          { name: "Treating a 72% Margin as a Safe Supermajority Requiring Minimal Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72% result is not a supermajority guarantee but a coalition that requires continuous activation and delivery — particularly in a district with 49.6% renters, high minority population, and younger median age where turnout variability is significant. The incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 72% is durable without active constituent engagement and legislative delivery on housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity is assuming away the demobilization risk that is the actual vulnerability in this district; any challenger who mobilizes the renter constituency around housing affordability or the minority communities around economic opportunity could shift margins substantially." }
