          { name: "Workforce Development & Local Job Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 15.1% college attainment and median income significantly below state averages, the district's economic future depends on job creation and workforce training that leads to sustainable local employment rather than out-migration. Eastern Kentucky has experienced persistent brain drain as younger adults leave for regional labor markets with higher wages; workforce development tied to specific employer needs, apprenticeship programs, and infrastructure investment that attracts employers can demonstrate tangible economic progress. A representative who can articulate a specific vision for how the district generates jobs and retains working-age population builds credibility with a constituency under real economic pressure." },
          { name: "Renter Community Economic Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.6% of the district, renters represent meaningful economic stratification in a low-income rural community where housing cost burdens are likely acute and mobility is limited. Renters in this income bracket face particular vulnerability to healthcare costs, transportation expenses, and childcare burdens that compound their economic precarity. While the district's homeowner majority may not prioritize renter-specific issues, ignoring the renter constituency's needs creates latent electoral vulnerability if opposition emerges and activates renters around housing affordability and economic justice messaging." },
          { name: "Taking Unopposed Status as Permanence", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% unopposed victory in 2024 reflects successful consolidation of the Republican base in a deeply red district, not a durable structural advantage. If material conditions deteriorate—rural hospital closures, rising healthcare costs, local employer losses—or if an energized challenger emerges with authentic constituent relationships and a focused economic message, the absence of current opposition provides no protection. Treating the unopposed status as a signal to govern less visibly or to neglect constituent service in low-income communities is the quickest path to creating conditions for a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Dismissing the Economic Concerns of Non-Homeowners", tag: "avoid", why: "With 78.4% homeownership, the district's homeowner majority is the dominant electoral force, but the 21.6% renter minority—in a $46,820-income, low-education-attainment district—faces acute economic vulnerability that can become a mobilizing issue if a challenger frames the incumbent as indifferent to renters' needs. Rental affordability, tenant protections, and economic opportunity for non-asset-holding households are real governance questions in a district where economic stratification is visible and material. Allowing renters to feel invisible in constituent service invites opposition that targets this constituency directly." }
