          { name: "Distinguishing Unopposed Status from Electoral Durability", tag: "careful", why: "Lewis's unopposed 2024 victory is a significant political asset, but one that can mask underlying vulnerabilities in a district with this income profile and education level. Unopposed status typically reflects either durable partisan strength or the absence of organized opposition infrastructure — in economically distressed districts, it is more often the latter. Any shift in state economic conditions, emergence of a credible opposition candidate, or failure to demonstrate legislative record on economic priorities could quickly convert current dominance into genuine competition." },
          { name: "Overreliance on Partisan Identity as an Electoral Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "In low-income districts where material economic stress is the dominant lived experience, campaigns that rely primarily on partisan messaging and inherited voter loyalty without anchoring to tangible legislative record on economic concerns are vulnerable to persuadable voters and sudden electoral swings. Unopposed victory in 2024 does not guarantee unopposed victory in a future cycle where opposition is organized around economic grievances." },
          { name: "Neglect of Constituent Service & Accessibility", tag: "avoid", why: "In economically constrained districts where households regularly face navigation challenges around healthcare, housing, utilities, and public benefits, constituent service infrastructure and representative accessibility are trust-building foundations. A representative whose office is difficult to reach or unresponsive to individual household problems will find that trust degrades rapidly, creating vulnerability to opposition campaigns that emphasize accessibility and responsiveness." }
