          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security and Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district is 76.5% homeowner with a $78,518 median household income — a constituency of asset-holding but not extraordinarily wealthy households for whom property taxes, school district quality, and neighborhood stability are the primary economic concerns that drive voting behavior. A legislator who demonstrates specific fluency on property tax assessment fairness, school funding mechanisms tied to property wealth, and neighborhood investment infrastructure commands credibility with the demographic that produces the 68% Republican result. This is not an abstract issue for affluent suburbanites; it is the material baseline governance expectation for three-quarters of the district's households." },
          { name: "Healthcare Affordability and Access for Established Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.6-year median age places this district in the cohort where healthcare costs become a primary household budget concern — particularly for self-insured families, small business owners, and those approaching Medicare eligibility. Insurance costs, prescription drug pricing, and rural healthcare access (if the district includes non-metropolitan areas) are direct household economic issues, not abstract policy debates. A candidate who can articulate specific solutions to healthcare cost containment while respecting market-based approaches demonstrates they understand the district's actual economic concerns and are not simply applying partisan ideology." },
          { name: "Understanding the Democratic 32% and Whether It Is Growing", tag: "careful", why: "One-third of the district voted Democratic in 2024, and the strategic question is whether this represents a stable minority of renters and college-educated professionals skeptical of Republican governance, or an emerging coalition that could shift the seat if current trends continue. The 29.1% college-attainment rate and 23.5% renter population together represent roughly 35-40% of the district's demographic composition, and if Democratic performance with these groups is stronger than with homeowners, the 68% margin may be less durable than it appears. Any candidate seeking to hold the seat must actively understand what Democratic voters are responding to and whether their message is consolidating Republican homeowners while actually losing ground among college-educated professionals or younger renters." },
          { name: "Treating the 68% as Inevitable or Durable Without Examining Its Actual Foundation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68-32 result suggests stability, but in a district where one-third of voters rejected the Republican candidate, assuming that margin reflects permanent coalition alignment rather than specific policy performance or incumbent strength is a strategic error. If the Democratic vote is concentrated among college-educated professionals frustrated with education policy or among renters struggling with housing costs, the 68% may evaporate if the incumbent changes or if a challenger addresses those constituencies' concerns with specificity. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 68% is a durable floor rather than an earned result from specific policy alignment with the district's majority will discover mid-campaign that the margin is less secure than the topline suggests." },
          { name: "Positioning Renters or Low-Income Households as Outside the District's Character or Legitimate Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The 23.5% renter population represents real households in the district with genuine economic concerns on housing affordability, tenant protections, and the cost of living — concerns that are not partisan or abstract but material and immediate. Any candidate whose messaging or legislative record positions renters as a special interest or frames their concerns as outside the district's priorities will find that framing actively hostile to the lived reality of one-quarter of the district's residents. Engaging renters and lower-income households as constituencies whose concerns are legitimate and addressable is both a governance obligation and a political necessity in a district where one-third of voters chose Democratic representation." }
