          { name: "Rural Infrastructure & Service Decline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.8% college attainment and $47,890 income in a district with a 42.5 median age signal a rural, non-metropolitan community where the erosion of local services—hospitals, banks, schools, retail—has direct economic impact. The viability of rural broadband, emergency services, and the economic anchors that keep small towns viable are not generic Republican or Democratic issues; they are the foundation of whether young adults stay and whether older adults can age in place. A legislator who can point to specific rural infrastructure investments, service retention, or economic development work commands stronger constituent loyalty than one who defaults to partisan abstraction." },
          { name: "Latino and Black Community Visibility & Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "With 3.37% Hispanic and 0.94% Black population, these communities represent roughly 4.3% of the district and are often absorbed into broader 'minority outreach' without specific attention to their distinct economic concerns. In a low-income district (median $47,890), Latino and Black households likely face specific barriers to economic mobility, healthcare access, and job development that are distinct from the white working-class majority. Recruiting, retaining, or flipping these voters requires demonstrating that the representative understands and prioritizes their specific economic mobility concerns, not token acknowledgment; the cost of invisibility is that a future challenger with targeted messaging and ground presence can shift these voters' baseline expectations." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as a Durable Electoral Foundation", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 reflects the current absence of Democratic challenger investment, not the presence of structural Republican strength. In a district with $47,890 median income and aging homeowners facing healthcare cost pressure, any Democrat who invests in the seat with concrete economic messaging has the foundation for a credible challenge—particularly if the incumbent is perceived as inattentive to household economic security concerns. Confusing unopposed outcome with safe re-election in a future cycle when Democratic recruitment may improve is a high-risk strategic error." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Abstraction Rather Than Concrete Economic Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "A district at $47,890 median income with a 42.5 median age and 83% non-college education evaluates elected officials on whether their policies improve household economic security and reduce material cost burden—not on inherited partisan identity or abstract ideological positioning. A representative who campaigns and governs on partisan messaging rather than specific healthcare affordability, property tax, rural infrastructure, or wage-supporting job development work will find that a future challenger with concrete economic answers commands credibility that the incumbent cannot overcome. In low-income rural districts, material concerns dominate electoral calculus." }
