          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Property-Based Wealth", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 79.7% homeownership implied by the 40.3% renter rate, KY-SD-22 is a property-owner-dominant constituency where residential equity, property tax rates, and neighborhood stability are direct voter concerns. At $66,310 median household income and a 36.7-year median age, homeowners here have accumulated significant assets and evaluate governance on whether their property investment is secure and appreciating. Property tax fairness, local school funding (which affects property values), and public safety are the concrete governance issues that consolidate the Republican base—and they are also the issues most vulnerable to neglect if a legislator focuses exclusively on statewide or national priorities rather than district-specific constituent concerns." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Education Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 35.8% college-educated adults, KY-SD-22 has the highest education attainment level for districts analyzed in this cohort—a signal that professional and credentialed workers form a significant portion of the electorate and that education funding, higher education access, and workforce development are not peripheral issues. This constituency cares about schools, university system viability, and whether government is investing in the intellectual infrastructure that drives professional opportunity. A Republican legislator who speaks fluently to education investment—not as an adversary to business interests but as an essential public good—commands credibility with this demographic; conversely, a legislator perceived as indifferent to education funding opens this constituency to Democratic challenge on the specific grounds that Republicans are neglecting professional-class interests." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 40.3% renters, KY-SD-22 has a substantial cohort without property-based wealth accumulation whose economic interests diverge materially from homeowners—and this renter constituency likely skews younger, less affluent, and more Democratic than the homeowner majority. Housing affordability, healthcare costs, and economic mobility are the primary concerns for renters at this income level; they are also the issues most likely to drive the 40% Democratic baseline and to shift further Democratic if a Republican incumbent is perceived as indifferent to renter economic concerns. Any candidate can consolidate homeowners, but holding or growing this seat requires a credible answer to why the renter constituency should support Republican governance on their material priorities." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Stable Coalition Rather Than a Coalition Under Pressure", tag: "avoid", why: "The 60-40 result is not a comfortable majority in a district with a $66,310 median income and 35.8% college education—it is a Republican hold that depends on consistent turnout and persuasion among a college-educated, younger, partially-renting constituency that is nationally volatile. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as safely Republican and assumes the homeowner base will hold regardless of whether government is delivering on professional-class and renter concerns will find the margin eroding. The 40% opposition is baked into the district's demographic composition, meaning it is not residual or swing—it is structural, and it grows if the incumbent neglects college-educated professionals or younger renters." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost as Secondary to Property Tax or Ideology", tag: "avoid", why: "At $66,310 median household income and 36.7-year median age, KY-SD-22's constituency includes substantial numbers of employed adults with healthcare concerns—both through employer plans and personal anxiety about rising costs. If a Republican legislator is perceived as prioritizing ideology-driven healthcare policy over constituent cost management or access concerns, this opens immediate political vulnerability among college-educated professionals and younger workers who value pragmatic solutions over partisan positioning. Any candidate who frames healthcare primarily through ideological opposition to government programs, rather than through constituent cost-of-care concerns, is ceding persuadable voters in this district to Democratic challengers." }
