          { name: "Property Tax Fairness and Fiscal Responsibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30.5% renters but 69.5% homeowners, the homeowning majority in KY-SD-24 has direct financial stakes in property tax policy, assessment fairness, and whether government spending is efficient and necessary. At a $73,853 median income, these are not wealthy households with unlimited tax capacity; they are established professionals whose net worth is concentrated in home equity. Property tax management and fiscal responsibility at the state and local level are the tangible governance issues that either earn incumbent loyalty or create opening for opposition on fiscal grounds." },
          { name: "Minority Community Representation and Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.2% Black and 2.73% Hispanic communities are small but present in the district, and the 62-38 partisan result may underestimate either community's electoral impact or the degree to which they feel represented in incumbent governance. Economic mobility, healthcare access, and whether Black and Hispanic residents experience equitable service delivery are the constituent concerns that can either consolidate Democratic support or represent untapped persuadable voters if an incumbent has built genuine relationships. In a 62-38 seat, modest shifts in minority community turnout or preference can narrow the margin materially." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Over Local Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 Republican margin in a district this affluent and professionally educated suggests that the opposition is not driven primarily by alignment with statewide or national Democratic platform but rather by specific local concerns — education funding, tax policy, local services quality. Any Republican candidate who campaigns on partisan red-meat issues unrelated to the district's material governance challenges will consolidate only the existing coalition and risk being outflanked by a Democrat who brings local focus. Similarly, any Democrat who campaigns primarily on national partisan messaging rather than KY-SD-24's actual education, tax, and service delivery concerns will not be credible with the moderate professionals whose votes determine the margin." },
          { name: "Treating the Affluent Professional Base as Guaranteed", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated professionals at $73,853 income are not ideologically locked into either party — they evaluate candidates on outcomes, fiscal competence, and whether their material interests (education quality, property value protection, tax efficiency) are being served. Any incumbent who takes the 62% result as permanent rather than the product of ongoing delivery on those concrete concerns risks losing persuadable voters to a credible challenger who makes a compelling case on local governance. This is a constituency that will switch if convinced the incumbent is failing on the issues that directly affect their quality of life." }
