          { name: "Educated Tax-Policy Conservatism Among the 40% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "The 40% Republican share in a district with 50.6% college attainment and $80,922 median income suggests a cohort of educated, higher-income voters choosing Republican candidates on grounds of tax policy, regulatory burden, or fiscal conservatism rather than cultural conservatism. These voters are not persuadable on the basis of social issues or identity politics; they are evaluating state tax policy and the regulatory environment for small business or professional practice. Berg's margin will hold if Democratic delivery on urban quality-of-life issues demonstrably outweighs Republican messaging on taxes—but if the opposition can credibly frame Democratic control as translating into higher costs for educated professionals, the 60-40 margin is vulnerable." },
          { name: "Assuming 60% is a Stable Mandate for Partisan Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in an affluent, educated district is a consolidated Democratic base, but it is not a mandate to govern without regard to the 40% or to pursue partisan legislative agendas that alienate educated swing voters. In Kentucky, where statewide politics remain competitive, a state senator who ignores educated urban voters' quality-of-life priorities in favor of cultural-war positioning or abstract left-wing causes will find the margin narrowing; Berg's success depends on maintaining the perception that Democratic governance delivers material results for affluent urbanites, not that it pursues partisan advantage." },
          { name: "Neglecting Constituent Service & Local Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district composed primarily of college-educated professionals with $80k+ median income, constituent expectations for legislative responsiveness and local accountability are high. Voters in this demographic typically have the resources to evaluate multiple candidates and to switch support if they perceive incumbent neglect or detachment. Berg's 60% margin will hold only if the office demonstrates active constituent service, regular engagement with the district, and responsiveness to local concerns—not just statewide partisan positioning. A competitor who can credibly position themselves as more locally engaged will have an opening to contest the seat." }
