          { name: "Rural Economic Security for Aging, Lower-Income Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $43,210 median income, 42.4 median age, and 25.6% renter rate, KY-SD-31 is a constituency of aging, financially constrained households where fixed costs — healthcare, housing, utilities — are rising faster than incomes. Social Security adequacy, Medicare access and prescription drug affordability, and rural hospital viability are not abstract policy issues but direct survival concerns for the majority of constituents. A representative who speaks with specificity about these issues and delivers legislative results on them — expanded Medicare coverage, prescription drug cost controls, rural healthcare infrastructure — builds durable constituent relationships that make the seat genuinely secure rather than only nominally so." },
          { name: "Job Creation and Economic Diversification in Post-Industrial Appalachia", tag: "lean-into", why: "A median income under $43,300 and college attainment of 14.7% in a district this size suggest limited economic opportunity and potential dependence on declining or seasonal employment. Manufacturing, coal, and extractive industries have contracted across Appalachia, and the incumbent must address what economic activities will support working families if those traditional sectors continue to shrink. Workforce development, broadband infrastructure for remote work, small business support, and attraction of light manufacturing or service-sector employers are concrete legislative priorities that signal engagement with the district's fundamental economic challenge rather than passive acceptance of decline." },
          { name: "Housing Stability and Renter Protection in a Constrained Market", tag: "careful", why: "With 25.6% of households renting and median income of $43,210, a meaningful portion of KY-SD-31 residents face genuine housing instability and vulnerability to rent increases that can consume 40-50% of income. Rental assistance, tenant protections, and fair housing enforcement are not 'urban' or partisan issues — they are material concerns for a quarter of the district's households. The incumbent must engage this constituency with specific policy proposals rather than dismissing housing as an urban concern, because renters in rural Kentucky face the same squeeze as renters anywhere; failing to address it can create an opening for a challenger who does." },
          { name: "Using Unopposed Status as Justification for Reduced Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 creates a false sense of security and risks weakening the incumbent's constituent-service infrastructure, direct engagement schedule, and ability to hear emerging community problems before opposition exploits them. An unopposed race does not mean there is no opposition — it means no one ran against you yet. If the incumbent uses the unopposed victory to reduce visibility, let the constituent-service operation atrophy, or appear indifferent to economic hardship, the groundwork for a future challenge is already laid." },
          { name: "Dismissing Lower-Income or Renter Constituencies as Non-Republican or Non-Constituents", tag: "avoid", why: "KY-SD-31's 25.6% renter rate and sub-$44,000 median income represent actual constituents with real material needs around housing, healthcare, and economic stability — not outsiders or special interests. An incumbent who frames these as fringe concerns or treats lower-income renters as marginal to the district's 'real' base (older homeowners) will find that dismissal translating into reduced turnout, reduced goodwill, and potential openness to a challenger. In a district this economically constrained, treating struggling constituents as legitimate is both a moral obligation and a strategic necessity." }
